### =============================================================
### OWASP A05 – Security Misconfiguration
### =============================================================
### Two problems:
###   (a) A debug endpoint leaks the JWT secret and every config value
###   (b) CORS wildcard (AllowAnyOrigin) – visible in the response headers
### =============================================================

@baseUrl = http://localhost:5218


### --- (a) Debug endpoint: leak the complete configuration ---
# No auth, no rate limiting – it returns:
#   - Jwt:Secret ("aviato-jwt-signing-key-2024-prod!")
#   - ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT
#   - EVERY configuration value as a dictionary – including EVERY environment
#     variable of the shell that started the API
#
# ⚠️ Those are YOUR environment variables. If you have API keys or tokens
#    exported in your shell, they are in the response – don't screen-share it.
#
# In production this would leak connection strings, API keys and secrets.
GET {{baseUrl}}/debug/config

###

### --- (b) CORS wildcard: preflight request from a foreign origin ---
# AllowAnyOrigin() means: any website, in the user's browser, may call this API.
# The browser sends an OPTIONS preflight before the real request.
# A secure API would not allow this origin (it isn't on the allow-list).
# Actual: 204 No Content with Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
OPTIONS {{baseUrl}}/products
Origin: https://evil-attacker.com
Access-Control-Request-Method: GET
Access-Control-Request-Headers: Authorization

###

### --- Check: the CORS header on a real request ---
# Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * lets the browser read cross-origin responses.
# That lets e.g. a phishing page call the API in the logged-in user's context
# and read the answer.
GET {{baseUrl}}/products
Origin: https://evil-attacker.com
